We need to build complete retrosynthesis route JSON. Inputs: we have three templates and their precursors.

We have target molecule? Not explicitly given but from apply_template answers we have precursors for each template applied to the molecule (the target). The target molecule likely is the product of those templates. The apply_template-1 gave precursors: ['COc1cccc(NC(=O)c2nn[nH]c2N)c1', 'FC1CN(Cc2ccc(CCl)cc2)C1'].

So target molecule is something that when applying template 1914393 yields those two precursors. The mapped_rxn shows product side (right side) is product with many groups. The product SMILES is the right side of mapped_rxn. Let's extract product SMILES from mapped_rxn of template 1914393: after >>. The mapped_rxn string: "[CH3:1][O:2][c:3]1...>>[CH3:1][O:2][c:3]1...". So product is the right side: "[CH3:1][O:2][c:3]1[cH:4][cH:5][cH:6][c:7]([NH:8][C:9](=[O:10])[c:11]2[n:12][n:13][n:14]([CH2:15][c:16]3[cH:17][cH:18][c:19]([CH2:20][N:21]4[CH2:22][CH:23]([F:24])[CH2:25]4)[cH:26][cH:27]3)[c:28]2[NH2:29])[cH:30]1"

That's the target product. Similarly template 1914394 product is right side: "[F:1][CH:2]1[CH2:3][N:4]([CH2:5][c:6]2[cH:7][cH:8][c:9]([CH2:10][Cl:11])[cH:12][cH:13]2)[CH2:14]1". That's maybe an intermediate? Actually apply_template-2 gave precursors ['ClCCl', 'FC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1'] for that template applied to the same target? Wait apply_template-2 used template 1914394 on the same molecule (the target). So product is same target, precursors are those two.

Similarly template 1914395 product is "[F:1][CH:2]1[CH2:3][N:4]([CH2:5][c:6]2[cH:7][cH:8][cH:9][cH:10][cH:11]2)[CH2:12]1". Apply_template-3 gave precursors ['OC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1'].

Thus we have a hierarchical route: target molecule (product) can be broken via three different reactions? Actually they are sequential steps: maybe first reaction (template 1914393) splits into two fragments: fragment A = COc1cccc(NC(=O)c2nn[nH]c2N)c1 (that's a phenoxy amide) and fragment B = FC1CN(Cc2ccc(CCl)cc2)C1 (a fluorinated morpholine-like). Then further each fragment can be broken: fragment B can be broken via template 1914394 into ClCCl and FC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1. Then that fragment can be broken via template 1914395 into OC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1 (maybe further). Actually template 1914395 applied to the same target gave precursor OC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1 (single). Wait that seems inconsistent: template 1914395 product is a smaller molecule (no Cl, no F?). Actually product of 1914395 is same as product of 1914394? Let's compare: product of 1914394 includes a chlorine substituent on aromatic ring. product of 1914395 is same but without chlorine? Actually product of 1914395: "[F:1][CH:2]1[CH2:3][N:4]([CH2:5][c:6]2[cH:7][cH:8][cH:9][cH:10][cH:11]2)[CH2:12]1". That's a fluorinated heterocycle attached to phenyl (no Cl). So maybe template 1914395 is a later step after removal of Cl from the aromatic ring (dechlorination). The precursor from apply_template-3 is OC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1 (hydroxy analog). So route: target -> via 1914395 -> precursor OC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1. Then maybe that precursor can be further broken? Not given.

But we need to build complete route of chemicals based on inputs. Likely we need to construct a JSON tree with root target molecule (product SMILES from template 1914393). Then children: reaction node with template_id 1914393, children molecules: the two precursors from apply_template-1. For each of those precursors, if we have further reactions (for the second precursor), we add reaction nodes.

Second precursor (FC1CN(Cc2ccc(CCl)cc2)C1) can be broken via template 1914394 into ClCCl and FC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1. Then that product (FC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1) can be broken via template 1914395 into OC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1. So we have a chain.

First precursor (COc1cccc(NC(=O)c2nn[nH]c2N)c1) has no further steps given.

Thus final route JSON.

We need to ensure template_id fields are numbers? In example they used integer. Our template IDs are 1914393 etc. Use as integer.

Construct JSON:

{
 "type":"mol",
 "smiles":"[CH3:1][O:2][c:3]1[cH:4][cH:5][cH:6][c:7]([NH:8][C:9](=[O:10])[c:11]2[n:12][n:13][n:14]([CH2:15][c:16]3[cH:17][cH:18][c:19]([CH2:20][N:21]4[CH2:22][CH:23]([F:24])[CH2:25]4)[cH:26][cH:27]3)[c:28]2[NH2:29])[cH:30]1",
 "children":[
   {
     "type":"reaction",
     "template_id":1914393,
     "children":[
        {"type":"mol","smiles":"COc1cccc(NC(=O)c2nn[nH]c2N)c1"},
        {"type":"mol","smiles":"FC1CN(Cc2ccc(CCl)cc2)C1","children":[
           {
             "type":"reaction",
             "template_id":1914394,
             "children":[
                {"type":"mol","smiles":"ClCCl"},
                {"type":"mol","smiles":"FC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1","children":[
                   {
                     "type":"reaction",
                     "template_id":1914395,
                     "children":[
                        {"type":"mol","smiles":"OC1CN(Cc2ccccc2)C1"}
                     ]
                   }
                ]}
             ]
           }
        ]}
     ]
   }
 ]
}

Check schema: each molecule can have children (reactions). That's allowed.

Now we should verify route schema using verify_route tool to ensure correct. Let's call it.
